1 NAME Lars "Lewis" /Reiersen Hauge/
2 GIVN Lars "Lewis"
2 _MARNM Reiersen Hauge
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Cambridge
3 STAE Iowa
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 23 AUG 1841
2 ADDR
3 STAE Rogaland fylke
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 APR 1917
2 ADDR
3 STAE Iowa
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/44337388/lewis-ryerson
2 CONT * Klepp Gards- og ættesoga gjenom 400 år, 1519-1900, Hauge, side 117, nr. 13.d
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Klepp Gards- og ættesoga gjenom 400 år, 1519-1900, Hauge, side 117, nr. 13'''
2 CONT Reier Eilefson, son åt nr. 10, f. 29/5 1803, d. 2/6 1876,
2 CONT G. 31/3 1830 m. Johanna Larsd., Storhaug nr. 7b, f. 5/6 1808, d. 13/5 1875.
2 CONT Born:
2 CONT * a. Ane, f. 30/11 1830, g. m. Rasmus Rasmusson, Årsvoll 76, Høyland.
2 CONT * b. Eilef, f. 17/12 1834, nr. 14.
2 CONT * c. Birgitte, f. 22/7 1838, g. m. nr. 19, g. 2. m. Ola Hansson, Orre nr. 58.
2 CONT * d. Lars, f. 23/8 1841, USA.
2 CONT * e. Reinert, f. 31/3 1849, USA.
2 CONT Reier fekk skøyte på 19 sp. 21/3 1831, 1846 gjekk det frå 1/3 til br. nr. 1, og skylda vart då 5,13 mark, førd til 1860.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Ane Elizabeth Ryerson was born in Norway on July 6, 1846. She came to America when 17 years old. Her brother John cried when her parents gave her permission to cross the Atlantic, so it was finally decided to let him make the trip
2 CONC  with her. They came to the Rasmus Lewis home, northeast of Cambridge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As was customary in those days for girls who wanted to do housework, she did housework in Des Moines. She often told her children how homesick she was for Norway and her parents. When her employer would call her in the morning, be
2 CONC ing half asleep she would think it was the voice of her mother calling her. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Lewis Ryerson. The wedding was held at the home of her brother-in-law and sister Mr. and Mrs. Rasmus Lewis. The Rev. Osmond Sheldahl officiated at the ceremony. Immediately after being pronounced man and wife, the husb
2 CONC and said, “Naa er du me” (Now you are mine). They bought government land for $1.25 an acre in Union Township, about 4 miles northwest of Cambridge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To them were born ten children, six girls and four boys. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Rinard Johan, born 1867. Rinard married Bertha Kalsem and after her death, he married Clara Barger. Rinard died May 1, 1944, at the age of 77. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Ane Mathilda, born July 6, 1868. She married Arthur Anderson. He died leaving her with two small children, Arthur and Anna. Mathilda died on her 48th birthday, in 1916. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Johanna Lovisa (Hannah), born June 16, 1870, married Charlie Heggen. They had one daughter and five sons. Hannah died April 11, 1929, at the age of 58. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Alfred Levi was born July 15, 1872, married Minnie Lackous, had one son and passed away in 1937 at the age of 64. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Jonna Lorrendi (Jennie) was born March 11, 1874, married Louis Roe, had two daughters and three sons. Passed away March 3, 1946, age 75. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Ephraim Miller, born April 21, 1876, married Helen Nervig, had one daughter and six sons. Passed away February 1, 1915, age 38 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Lisa Marie (Lizzy), born August 5, 1878, married Albert Hill, had one son, Leslie. Died December 12, 1907, age 29 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Bertha Karina (Carrie), born March 27, 1881, married John Larson, had two daughters. Passed away February 4, 1943, age 61. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Oscar Lewis, born June 13, 1883, married Liza Eide, had six sons and one daughter. Passed away September 14, 1938, age 55 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Malinda Sofie (Mollie) born 1885, July 11, never married. Died July 11, 1910. 
2 CONT  	          Lewis Ryerson (Grandpa) was born August 23, 1841. Died April 24, 1917, age 75. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children attended school at the old brick schoolhouse in Union Township. Some of the teachers were Clara Aplain from Cambridge, now of Ames; Annie Corkin, now Mrs. Al Lee; Nellie Jeweet, now Mrs. L. L. Wherry; Lew Butterson; a
2 CONC nd Mrs. Mose Lee. In this school, wall maps, globes or dictionaries were rare articles. The stove was called the Golden Egg, and on cold mornings, it was just that clear up to the stovepipe. On a blizzardy morning, the chalk just 
2 CONC would travel along the floor and many a class would recite while standing around the stove. The teacher was expected to have the house warm by the time the pupils came. The wise teacher brought in coal and kindling and emptied the
2 CONC  drinking water out of the pail before before he or she left at night.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT The schools measured themselves against each other by spelling school and ciphering matches. McGuffeys Readers, Rays or Robinsons’s Arithmetic were the popular text books. The law said that the director should visit the school twi
2 CONC ce each term. But generally he just saw to it that there was coal, kindling, chalk and erasers, and a broom, and felt that he had done his duty. In the early 80’s practically no country child expected to go to high school. The non
2 CONC -professional teachers taught the country schools.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ryerson family attended the Palestine Church where the children were baptized and confirmed. In later years, they tranferred to the Cambridge Lutheran Church.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT On account of the long distance and poor transportation, the children attended Sunday School which was organized at the Person Schoolhouse by Mr. Person. It was quite non-sectarian and lasted for several years. Mrs. Clarence Pears
2 CONC on and Mrs. Nettie Kelley were some of the teachers.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT The family’s first house was a log cabin built by the father. It was composed of a kitchen, living room, small-bedroom, and one room upstairs which was reached by a ladder leading to a trap door in the floor overhead. Straw sheds 
2 CONC were used for the stock.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT In those days there was no mail delivery. The owner would drive to Cambridge once a week for mail which was put in grain sacks. The usual mail was the “Scandinavian News” and “Decorah Posten”. There were no farm papers in those da
2 CONC ys.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT In snowy and stormy weather, the trip was often post-poned for six or seven weeks, which meant they would run out of provisions. Cloths were dipped in lard for light, which answered the purpose of torches or lamps. Perhaps sweet p
2 CONC otatoes were roasted and ground for coffee.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT Their winter evenings were spent playing dominoes and checkers, popping corn (not popcorn, but parched corn), and making candy from the big barrel of sorghum. 	
2 CONT
2 CONT As they grew up, the girls did housework and the boys helped with farm work at home. Rinard tiled. Ephraim rented the A. K. Johnson farm. Alfred worked by the month on farms near by. Oscar took over the responsibility of the farm 
2 CONC work when his father was no longer able to do so.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT In this home were raised to womanhood and manhood Anna Anderson (Mrs. Seward Larson) and Arthur Anderson, children of Mathilda Anderson.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT They felt the need of money as most early settlers did to meet payment on the farm or to make improvements. They adjusted their spending to their income. They lived well as far as food went. There was wheat in the bin, pork in the
2 CONC  barrel or in the smoke house, a quarter of beef frozen in the winter season, a bin full of potatoes and one of apples, as well as sacks of dried fruit, and big jars of apples plum butter and pickles.  	
2 CONT
2 CONT Hunger seemed a long way off and starvation impossible. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  - “unknown author (seemingly a grandaughter of Lars and Ane). Transcribed unchanged from a five-page collection of papers found in Carrie (Ryerson) Larson’s daughter Elisabeth (Larson) Miller’s chest of photos passed down to her 
2 CONC daughter Betty (Miller) Zierke and finally to her son Bennett Zierke.” - Ryan Zierke (Bennett’s son) 
2 CONT
